WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.290 --> 00:00:01.290
They're rapists.

00:00:01.290 --> 00:00:04.090
And some, I assume, are good people.

00:00:04.090 --> 00:00:06.180
The idea of completely discounting Mexicans

00:00:06.180 --> 00:00:10.040
and talking about your second most important
trading partner in that way.

00:00:10.040 --> 00:00:13.320
We have some bad hombres here and we're going
to get 'em out.

00:00:13.320 --> 00:00:16.460
That's not the art of the deal, that is just
very bad business.

00:00:19.400 --> 00:00:20.920
Hi, I'm Nick Gillespie for Reason,

00:00:20.920 --> 00:00:22.960
and today we're talking with Roberto Salinas.

00:00:22.960 --> 00:00:25.240
He's the president of the Mexico Business
Forum,

00:00:25.240 --> 00:00:29.140
and he is a persistent and vocal critic of
Donald Trump.

00:00:29.150 --> 00:00:30.830
Roberto, thanks for talking to Reason.

00:00:30.830 --> 00:00:32.680
It's a great pleasure to be here, Nick.

00:00:32.680 --> 00:00:34.800
Let's talk about NAFTA first and your role
in it.

00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:38.860
Is NAFTA a good thing or a bad thing for Mexico,
Canada and the US?

00:00:38.860 --> 00:00:40.480
It's getting a lot of heat lately.

00:00:40.480 --> 00:00:42.880
In the early 1990s, we thought,

00:00:42.880 --> 00:00:46.180
"Well, wow! Mexico's already a story of trade liberalization.

00:00:46.180 --> 00:00:52.400
We're exporting 35 billion dollars a year
worth of products and so on.”

00:00:52.400 --> 00:00:55.780
Today that number is 365 billions,

00:00:55.780 --> 00:00:59.160
so from a trade perspective, and this is a
trade agreement,

00:00:59.160 --> 00:01:00.090
from a trade perspective,

00:01:00.090 --> 00:01:04.340
I don't think there's any question that NAFTA
has been a success in the sale side.

00:01:04.340 --> 00:01:07.140
And then you go to the purchase side, in other
words, imports,

00:01:07.150 --> 00:01:10.380
and you find that you're also importing a
vast amount.

00:01:10.380 --> 00:01:12.320
Guess where those imports come from?

00:01:12.320 --> 00:01:14.650
In about 80 percent, the United States.

00:01:14.650 --> 00:01:15.650
Yeah, exactly.

00:01:15.650 --> 00:01:17.159
So those in Indiana depend ...

00:01:17.159 --> 00:01:19.320
I mean, speaking of Mike Pence,

00:01:19.320 --> 00:01:23.040
does Indiana depend on jobs because of its
trade with Mexico?

00:01:23.040 --> 00:01:24.680
Does Ohio?

00:01:24.680 --> 00:01:25.380
Texas?

00:01:25.380 --> 00:01:27.480
You want to shut down NAFTA?

00:01:27.480 --> 00:01:30.440
That turns Texas into a democratic state overnight.

00:01:30.440 --> 00:01:34.100
Where do you think the animus against NAFTA,

00:01:34.100 --> 00:01:35.622
particularly in the United States, and I mean,

00:01:35.622 --> 00:01:37.520
this is something that Donald Trump ran on.

00:01:37.520 --> 00:01:39.840
It's also something that Bernie Sanders brought
up a lot,

00:01:39.840 --> 00:01:44.500
the idea that somehow free trade agreements
suck jobs out of America

00:01:44.500 --> 00:01:46.330
and they put them in third-world countries,

00:01:46.330 --> 00:01:48.710
which due to a lot of economic ignorance,

00:01:48.710 --> 00:01:52.189
often times they're talking about Mexico as
a third-world country, as well.

00:01:52.189 --> 00:01:55.250
Where does the resentment of something like
that come from?

00:01:55.250 --> 00:01:57.080
I think that was one of the great lessons

00:01:57.080 --> 00:01:59.860
of the Trump campaign, the Bernie Sanders
campaign.

00:01:59.860 --> 00:02:03.420
It's not something that discriminates between
Republicans and Democrats

00:02:03.420 --> 00:02:06.170
or between the right or the left or whatever.

00:02:06.170 --> 00:02:09.399
What you found out is that there is anger
because there's displacement.

00:02:09.399 --> 00:02:12.209
There is job displacement, and that's a very
serious concern.

00:02:12.209 --> 00:02:14.769
But are we going to address it by closing
our economies?

00:02:14.769 --> 00:02:15.799
By building walls?

00:02:15.799 --> 00:02:20.049
I mean, wasn't another famous Republican the
one who said, "Tear down this wall."

00:02:20.049 --> 00:02:23.969
The same one that said, "We will always keep
our doors open in the shiny city on the hill

00:02:23.969 --> 00:02:25.810
no matter how many walls it may have."

00:02:25.810 --> 00:02:28.930
I think those principles have to be kept in
mind, but also,

00:02:28.930 --> 00:02:34.719
to be very serious about trying to alleviate
the disruption and the displacement

00:02:34.719 --> 00:02:39.920
that may come in this era of globalization
for those that have lost their jobs.

00:02:39.920 --> 00:02:41.440
you mentioned Indiana and Ohio.

00:02:41.440 --> 00:02:43.090
You can throw Michigan and Wisconsin

00:02:43.090 --> 00:02:45.659
states that were very important for Donald
Trump to win.

00:02:45.659 --> 00:02:49.540
It's clear that the anti-NAFTA message resonated
there.

00:02:49.540 --> 00:02:51.719
How do you help workers who may have been
displaced

00:02:51.719 --> 00:02:54.569
by the idea that certain types of industrial
jobs go elsewhere.

00:02:54.569 --> 00:02:55.569
Oh my goodness!

00:02:55.569 --> 00:02:56.569
That is a hard question.

00:02:56.569 --> 00:02:59.049
I think you would have to come in three tranches,

00:02:59.049 --> 00:03:01.499
but this is something purely speculative.

00:03:01.499 --> 00:03:07.280
The first is that you need some type of, hopefully,
market-oriented job loss insurance,

00:03:07.280 --> 00:03:10.769
that may be funded through the tremendous
economic exchange

00:03:10.769 --> 00:03:12.860
that is done on a trilateral basis,

00:03:12.860 --> 00:03:15.819
only on a temporary point of view, in other
words,

00:03:15.819 --> 00:03:19.299
as just a measure to alleviate the immediate
pain.

00:03:19.299 --> 00:03:21.059
But then you need something that's more structured,

00:03:21.059 --> 00:03:24.900
and that would have to be based on retraining,

00:03:24.900 --> 00:03:28.029
on education and on trilateral efforts,

00:03:28.029 --> 00:03:30.569
hopefully, more private-oriented than public-oriented,

00:03:30.569 --> 00:03:33.909
to try and find new workspaces for such people.

00:03:33.909 --> 00:03:37.510
And I think this is something that independently
of NAFTA is an issue in the United States

00:03:37.510 --> 00:03:40.829
in the next 10 years with the tremendous advances
of technologies,

00:03:40.829 --> 00:03:45.219
because those job displacements owe a lot
more

00:03:45.219 --> 00:03:48.209
to technological disruption and progress than
they due to trade.

00:03:48.209 --> 00:03:49.449
Right, yeah, I mean-

00:03:49.449 --> 00:03:53.799
And third world, by the way, the idea that
low wages are sucking ...

00:03:53.800 --> 00:03:57.800
It's more affordable and more productive worker
units.

00:03:57.800 --> 00:04:01.150
You go down to Hermosillo, Sonora, where they
have the Ford plant there,

00:04:01.150 --> 00:04:04.780
which has suspended production on one of its
Ford models,

00:04:04.780 --> 00:04:10.820
but otherwise Haiti would be the richest country
in Latin America.

00:04:10.820 --> 00:04:19.140
Otherwise, many of these sub-Saharan countries
would be by far the most prosperous ones.

00:04:19.140 --> 00:04:23.580
How does the Mexican business community view
somebody like Donald Trump?

00:04:23.590 --> 00:04:25.910
I mean, are they worried?

00:04:25.910 --> 00:04:28.580
Not just because of his rhetoric, but that
he'll do policies

00:04:28.580 --> 00:04:31.950
that actually do real damage to the ability
for the Mexican economy,

00:04:31.950 --> 00:04:33.840
or the North American economy to grow?

00:04:33.840 --> 00:04:38.000
Well, at first, there was a significant worry.

00:04:38.000 --> 00:04:39.830
We have to understand that NAFTA for Mexico

00:04:39.830 --> 00:04:42.710
was much more than a trade and an investment
agreement.

00:04:42.710 --> 00:04:46.120
It was importing the credibility of US institutions.

00:04:46.120 --> 00:04:49.370
If you do investment on a North American level,

00:04:49.370 --> 00:04:53.440
chances of a future government in Mexico trying
to derail that,

00:04:53.440 --> 00:04:56.430
as it happened to us before, are going to
be a lot harder.

00:04:56.430 --> 00:05:01.500
So that straitjacket effect perhaps is not
golden, but it certainly a silver straitjacket.

00:05:01.500 --> 00:05:04.100
Somebody may want to re-nationalize, re-expropriate
the banks

00:05:04.100 --> 00:05:08.250
or they may want to do all kinds of horrible
things in the populist tradition

00:05:08.250 --> 00:05:11.850
that has characterized many Latin American
countries.

00:05:11.850 --> 00:05:15.270
NAFTA was a great step forward in trying to,

00:05:15.270 --> 00:05:17.190
not preclude that completely,

00:05:17.190 --> 00:05:20.660
but significantly lessen the probability of
a future change,

00:05:20.660 --> 00:05:24.470
and that's what led to a great deal of investment
coming into Mexico.

00:05:24.470 --> 00:05:27.000
It was that credibility that was imported.

00:05:27.000 --> 00:05:33.330
In that sense, for Mexico the idea of abandoning
NAFTA ...

00:05:33.330 --> 00:05:37.300
We'll still trade with the United States at
an enormous level.

00:05:37.300 --> 00:05:41.900
Mexico today is the number one supplier of
auto parts in the United States,

00:05:41.900 --> 00:05:44.780
the number two supplier of automobiles in
the United States.

00:05:44.780 --> 00:05:48.040
NFL helmets are produced in Mexico, assembled
in Mexico.

00:05:48.040 --> 00:05:49.250
Pretty sure avocados.

00:05:49.250 --> 00:05:51.660
You guys have cornered the American market.

00:05:51.660 --> 00:05:56.720
The greatest lie, fake news ... Can't even
call them that.

00:05:56.720 --> 00:05:58.800
It's just an outright lie.

00:05:58.800 --> 00:06:02.210
The facts speak very differently about the
effect of NAFTA

00:06:02.210 --> 00:06:04.300
on Mexican agriculture, on the country.

00:06:04.300 --> 00:06:05.300
It's exploded.

00:06:05.300 --> 00:06:09.860
It's exploded, and I myself have friends
and many people that ...

00:06:09.860 --> 00:06:12.050
I don't know this from reading a statistic,

00:06:12.050 --> 00:06:16.410
I know this from actually visiting the different
farms, whether it's tomato ...

00:06:16.410 --> 00:06:19.120
You know, many of the tomatoes we eat in the
best restaurants

00:06:19.120 --> 00:06:22.370
in Las Vegas or in New York or in Los Angeles,
they come from Sinaloa.

00:06:22.370 --> 00:06:26.520
So you're saying Mexican tomatoes are taking
American tomatoes jobs?

00:06:26.520 --> 00:06:27.811
No, I'm not saying-

00:06:27.811 --> 00:06:28.811
You're selling Trump's idea.

00:06:28.811 --> 00:06:30.570
I'm saying that Donald Trump,

00:06:30.570 --> 00:06:33.500
when he goes and eats at Daniel Boulud, he
doesn't know ...

00:06:33.500 --> 00:06:38.680
In is a very expensive dinner, that he's actually
the one that's unemploying American ...

00:06:38.680 --> 00:06:40.050
Now, why isn't there room for both?

00:06:40.050 --> 00:06:44.070
Why don't you let consumers choose at the
end of the day?

00:06:44.070 --> 00:06:47.570
This leads, I think, to a great deal of fallacies
concerning the trade deficit.

00:06:47.570 --> 00:06:51.960
I mean, the intellectual backwardness of many
of Trump's trade advisors

00:06:51.960 --> 00:06:56.560
contrasts dramatically with some of the very
good advice he's gotten in terms of deregulation

00:06:56.560 --> 00:07:00.170
and in terms of taxes and in terms of draining
the swamp.

00:07:00.170 --> 00:07:04.180
When Donald Trump announced for the presidency,

00:07:04.180 --> 00:07:06.660
literally within five minutes he went on a
rant.

00:07:06.660 --> 00:07:09.770
They're bringing drugs, they're bringing crime.

00:07:09.770 --> 00:07:10.770
They're rapists.

00:07:10.770 --> 00:07:13.540
And some, I assume, are good people.

00:07:13.540 --> 00:07:19.180
What kind of anger or resentment does that
stir in you

00:07:19.190 --> 00:07:21.930
or in Mexicans broadly along with it,

00:07:21.930 --> 00:07:23.810
increasingly xenophobic rhetoric,

00:07:23.810 --> 00:07:27.220
both coming out of the Republican Party, but
many other parts of America?

00:07:27.220 --> 00:07:28.370
I'll tell you what it has done.

00:07:28.370 --> 00:07:31.260
It has empowered Andrés López Obrador,

00:07:31.260 --> 00:07:34.480
who is our equivalent of Hugo Chávez.

00:07:34.480 --> 00:07:38.650
A rabid, primitive, vitriolic populist.

00:07:38.650 --> 00:07:42.860
He's on the top for the 2018 presidential
election, today.

00:07:42.860 --> 00:07:46.930
He's leading on the polls, and that's because
he has ...

00:07:46.930 --> 00:07:51.190
Donald Trump became his number one campaign
advisor with that vitriol.

00:07:51.190 --> 00:07:53.110
So, instead of talking to each other,

00:07:53.110 --> 00:07:56.150
because that's usually what classical liberals
do,

00:07:56.150 --> 00:07:59.080
they learn how to talk to each other and listen
to each other,

00:07:59.080 --> 00:08:01.170
the idea of completely discounting Mexicans

00:08:01.170 --> 00:08:05.250
and talking about your second most important
trading partner in that vein,

00:08:05.250 --> 00:08:06.990
is just that's not the art of the deal.

00:08:06.990 --> 00:08:08.880
That is just very bad business.

00:08:08.880 --> 00:08:10.650
There's other ways of going around it.

00:08:10.650 --> 00:08:14.040
Look at the Republican Doug Ducey in Arizona,

00:08:14.040 --> 00:08:16.070
talking with Claudia Pavlovich in Sonora.

00:08:16.070 --> 00:08:18.419
They don't no longer want to distinguish the
states.

00:08:18.419 --> 00:08:19.710
They want to talk about the megaregion

00:08:19.710 --> 00:08:21.980
and how they can compliment each other's what?

00:08:21.980 --> 00:08:22.990
Comparative advantage?

00:08:22.990 --> 00:08:24.419
Has Donald Trump ever heard of that word?

00:08:24.419 --> 00:08:27.330
Has Peter Navarro ever read Adan Smith?

00:08:27.330 --> 00:08:28.480
David Ricardo?

00:08:28.480 --> 00:08:30.800
We're speaking at Freedom Fest in Las Vegas,

00:08:30.800 --> 00:08:35.029
the world's largest annual gathering of libertarians
and free market people.

00:08:35.029 --> 00:08:37.800
Two years ago, Donald Trump actually spoke
here

00:08:37.800 --> 00:08:40.099
and you had a heated exchange with him.

00:08:40.099 --> 00:08:41.680
You asked him a tough question.

00:08:41.680 --> 00:08:43.360
Recount for us what the question was,

00:08:43.360 --> 00:08:47.810
what his response was, and either do you feel
like you got through at all.

00:08:47.810 --> 00:08:50.680
Well no, actually, I felt rather humiliated.

00:08:50.680 --> 00:08:54.550
The man does know how to manage the stage.

00:08:54.550 --> 00:08:59.760
What I asked him was whether he would build
a wall in every single state

00:08:59.760 --> 00:09:04.160
within the United States to keep Nebraska
bad hombres out of Missouri

00:09:04.160 --> 00:09:08.509
and the bad element of Florida out of Washington
state,

00:09:08.509 --> 00:09:10.220
and what that would do to the economy,

00:09:10.220 --> 00:09:12.180
because by that logic, that's what you should
do

00:09:12.180 --> 00:09:15.290
and for that matter municipalities and whatnot.

00:09:15.290 --> 00:09:18.999
And so he said, "No," he would build the wall
just for Mexico

00:09:19.000 --> 00:09:21.840
and he accused me of being sent by the Mexican
government.

00:09:21.840 --> 00:09:25.060
Did the government of Mexico ask you to come
up here and make this?

00:09:25.060 --> 00:09:26.270
Oh of course, of course,

00:09:26.270 --> 00:09:28.800
Go ahead ask your question, ask your question,

00:09:28.800 --> 00:09:30.420
come on you got a lot of people waiting.

00:09:30.420 --> 00:09:31.960
Were you in fact sent by the Mexican government?

00:09:31.960 --> 00:09:32.940
Of course not.

00:09:32.940 --> 00:09:34.940
You were sent by the Russian government.

00:09:34.949 --> 00:09:39.160
Or the Haitian government or maybe the Tahiti.

00:09:39.160 --> 00:09:45.260
Do you feel like xenophobia is on the rise
in the NAFTA zone?

00:09:45.260 --> 00:09:51.319
I mean, are people in an age where economic
growth

00:09:51.320 --> 00:09:53.360
in many cases globally has been slowing down,

00:09:53.360 --> 00:09:56.220
certainly in the United States in the 21st
century,

00:09:56.220 --> 00:10:00.800
economic growth has been much lower that it
had been for the previous 50 or 60 years,

00:10:00.800 --> 00:10:03.749
is that breeding kind of resentment or a sense
of,

00:10:03.749 --> 00:10:05.889
"We're fighting over a shrinking pie,"

00:10:05.889 --> 00:10:09.760
and that, "We've got to keep out people who
we think aren't like us or can't become us"?

00:10:09.760 --> 00:10:11.680
That I think is a very important challenge.

00:10:11.689 --> 00:10:13.860
There's definitely an ingredient of xenophobia.

00:10:13.860 --> 00:10:18.540
I think we're still in time to curtail it.

00:10:18.540 --> 00:10:21.700
Mexican business people and the government
have learned

00:10:21.700 --> 00:10:26.820
that a lot of what Donald Trump says, a lot
of the vitriol and the venom are just that.

00:10:26.820 --> 00:10:31.680
They're rhetoric, and in reality we'll talk
about renegotiating NAFTA,

00:10:31.689 --> 00:10:33.079
but what does that entail?

00:10:33.079 --> 00:10:36.279
That may actually provide an opportunity to
develop, for instance,

00:10:36.279 --> 00:10:39.529
new technologies to allow trucks on both sides
of the border

00:10:39.529 --> 00:10:44.499
to cross, maybe a special bridge, after you
do full disclosure

00:10:44.499 --> 00:10:49.470
after you agree to be completely surveilled
by satellites and whatnot.

00:10:49.470 --> 00:10:52.040
And maybe that can even pay a little piece
of a wall

00:10:52.040 --> 00:10:55.410
that we'll still take 27 years to build or
some such,

00:10:55.410 --> 00:10:58.510
by the way, probably by Mexican workers and
Cemex, Mexican cements,

00:10:58.510 --> 00:11:00.760
so that may not be all bad for us.

00:11:00.760 --> 00:11:03.380
But it's the message is what hurts, is,

00:11:03.389 --> 00:11:04.389
"You're going to pay for it.

00:11:04.389 --> 00:11:08.610
I'm going to force you to pay for it."

00:11:08.610 --> 00:11:09.899
That's just not good diplomacy

00:11:09.899 --> 00:11:14.399
and that feeds that xenophobia north of the
border, but it also feeds it elsewhere.

00:11:14.399 --> 00:11:19.029
Donald Trump is not a highly regarded leader
of the free world

00:11:19.029 --> 00:11:21.120
and the rest of the world because of his antics

00:11:21.120 --> 00:11:23.410
and because of the reality TV that he lives
in

00:11:23.410 --> 00:11:29.350
and because of this fantasy, Freudian complex
that he might have,

00:11:29.350 --> 00:11:30.879
that he wants to be number one

00:11:30.879 --> 00:11:34.930
and he wants to tweet at every moment of the
day that he's number one.

00:11:34.930 --> 00:11:36.720
That's not how you're going to get things
done.

00:11:36.720 --> 00:11:42.910
That's not the art of the deal in statesmanship
and leadership in the global arena.

00:11:42.910 --> 00:11:45.269
We will leave it there, but the final question,
of course

00:11:45.269 --> 00:11:50.519
that I ask anybody from Mexico who is a classical
liberal or libertarian.

00:11:50.519 --> 00:11:52.899
Friedrich Hayek or Salma Hayek?

00:11:52.899 --> 00:11:53.899
Who's your favorite Hayek?

00:11:53.900 --> 00:11:54.820
Oh my goodness!

00:11:54.820 --> 00:11:56.480
That's an impossible question,

00:11:56.480 --> 00:11:58.730
but I would have to say Friedrich.

00:11:58.730 --> 00:12:00.600
Well, I want to thank Roberto Salinas.

00:12:00.600 --> 00:12:03.929
He is the president of the Mexico Business
Forum,

00:12:03.929 --> 00:12:06.519
for talking to us while we're at Freedom Fest
in Las Vegas.

00:12:06.520 --> 00:12:07.680
Roberto, thanks so much.

00:12:07.680 --> 00:12:08.680
Thank you, Nick.

00:12:08.740 --> 00:12:10.120
For Reason, I'm Nick Gillespie.

